<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonzalo Pizarro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1510–<a href="/years/1548">1548</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gonzalo Pizarro was a Spanish conquistador and younger half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>. He took part in the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> and led an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> in 1541. In 1544 he led the encomenderos' revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a>, killing Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blasco-nunez-vela">Blasco Núñez Vela</a> in 1546, but was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-la-gasca">Pedro de la Gasca</a> at Jaquijahuana in 1548 and executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro.jpg" alt="Gonzalo Pizarro" width="240" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gonzalo_Pizarro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gonzalo Pizarro was born in Trujillo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the illegitimate son of the infantry colonel Gonzalo Pizarro y Rodríguez de Aguilar, who had served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. He was the younger paternal half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro">Hernando Pizarro</a> and the older full brother of Juan Pizarro. In 1530 he accompanied Francisco on his third expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. After the capture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> and his execution, the Pizarro brothers took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> on 15 November 1533, and Gonzalo and Juan were made regidores of the city on 24 March 1534.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gonzalo and Juan ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> while Francisco founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1535. Their treatment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca Yupanqui</a>, including, by one account, Gonzalo's rape of his queen Cura Ocllo, helped provoke a large rebellion, and the Incas temporarily took Cuzco on 6 May 1536. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> imprisoned Gonzalo and Hernando on 8 April 1537, but Gonzalo escaped, and the brothers later defeated Almagro at Las Salinas.</p><p>In November 1539 Francisco made Gonzalo vice-governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and sent him to look for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a> and the Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">Cinnamon</a>. He left Quito in February 1541 with Spaniards and thousands of Native Americans, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-orellana">Francisco de Orellana</a> followed. The expedition ran out of food and many died. Gonzalo turned back with about 80 men, while Orellana went on to explore the length of the Amazon River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Almagristas killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> on 26 June 1541. In 1544 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blasco-nunez-vela">Blasco Núñez Vela</a> as the first viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a>, framed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> to protect Indigenous peoples. Conquistadors who would lose their hold on native labour backed Gonzalo and Francisco de Carvajal in the Great Encomendero Rebellion, and their army defeated Núñez at Añaquito, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, in 1546.</p><p>Carvajal urged Gonzalo to proclaim himself King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, but he refused. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-la-gasca">Pedro de la Gasca</a> then arrived with pardons and the repeal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a>, and most of Gonzalo's army deserted him before Jaquijahuana, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>. He surrendered and was beheaded on 10 April 1548. Werner Herzog's film Aguirre, the Wrath of God of 1972 reimagines his expeditions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gonzalo Pizarro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the same year <b>Gonzalo Pizarro</b> led about 300 Spaniards and many Andean allies into Vilcabamba and fought Manco’s men at Huayna Pucara.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first viceroy, Blasco Núñez Vela, arrived in 1544, but his attempt to enforce the New Laws provoked a revolt of encomenderos under <b>Gonzalo Pizarro</b>, who defeated and killed him at the Battle of Añaquito in 1546.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Returning from Chile in 1537, he seized Cusco from Hernando and <b>Gonzalo Pizarro</b> during the rebellion of Manco Inca.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-toledo">Governorate of New Toledo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Trujillo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-5.88&amp;year=1534" aria-label="Trujillo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gonzalo Pizarro"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4613 4184h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4613 4184h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-5.88&amp;year=1534">What was here in 1534? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
